'''Telephoridæ''' (tel-ẹ̄-for′i-dē), ''n.pl.'' [NL. (Leach, 1817), < ''Telephorus'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of serricorn beetles, including those forms commonly called ''soldier-beetles'', now usually merged with the ''Lampyridæ''. See ''Telephorinæ''. ''Malacodermidæ'' is a synonym.

'''Telephorinæ''' (tel″ẹ̄-fọ̄-rī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [< ''Telephorus'' + ''-inæ''.] The ''Telephoridæ'' as a subfamily of the ''Lampyridæ''. {{smaller|They have the middle coxæ contiguous and the epipleura distinct and narrow at base, and mesothoracic episterna not sinuate on the inner side. They are slender and rather soft-bodied beetles of medium size, usually vegetable-feeders, although carnivorous in the larval state. ''Chauliognathus'', ''Podabrus'', and ''Telephorus'' are the principal genera represented in the United States. See cut under ''soldier-beetle''.}}

'''Telephorus''' (tẹ̄-lef′ọ̄-rus), ''n.'' [NL. (Schaeffer, 1766), < Gr. τῆλε, afar, + -φορος, < φέρειν = E. ''bear''<sup>1</sup>.] A genus of serricorn beetles, typical of the family ''Telephoridæ''. {{smaller|It is of cosmopolitan distribution, and comprises more than 300 species, the majority of them inhabiting cold or temperate regions. Thirty-six species occur in the United States. ''T. bilineatus'', the two-lined soldier-beetle, is in its larval state, according to Riley, a common enemy of the larva of the codling-moth (''Carpocapsa pomonella''). See cut under ''soldier-beetle''.}}

